{"type":"message","id":"718ca74b","parentId":"7c4f48aa","timestamp":"2026-03-30T11:07:54.409Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the actual issue better:\n\n1. The error is: \"edit failed: Missing required parameters: path alias, oldText alias, newText alias. Supply correct parameters before retrying.\"\n2. The user ran an edit tool call with parameters, but got this error\n3. The system keeps retrying infinitely\n\nThe problem is NOT exactly what aoao described (SSE stream error). The actual problem is:\n1. User calls edit tool with some parameters\n2. The edit tool fails with \"Missing required parameters: path alias, oldText alias\"\n3. The system retries the same tool call with the same parameters\n4. The same error happens again → infinite loop\n\nThe root cause here is that the error message \"Missing required parameters\" suggests the parameters ARE being sent, but the server is rejecting them. The issue might be parameter formatting/encoding or the parameters are being corrupted during transmission.\n\nActually wait - the error says \"path alias, oldText alias, newText alias\" - these look like parameter names rather than values. This suggests the parameters ARE being sent as parameter names (like `path` as a key) rather than their actual values. This might be a JSON-RPC format issue where the tool call parameters are being serialized incorrectly.\n \nThe root cause is likely that the tool parameters are being serialized in the wrong format—the parameter names are being sent as literal values instead of their actual values. This looks like the same underlying issue I saw with #57334, so I should check if there's already a fix in place or if I need to apply the same solution here.","thinkingSignature":"5369148071d78f92cab670d3c12f40d2f9a1e661f4539464197a950f4e8fd7a8"},{"type":"text","text":"现在我理解了。问题是 **edit tool 的参数格式错误**：\"path alias, oldText alias, newText alias\" 说明参数名被当作值发送了，不是真正的参数值。\n\n这是参数序列化 bug，不是 SSE stream 问题。之前修过类似的 #57334。让我直接查：\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_nuvmvpt1jbxe_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/.openclaw/workspace/openclaw && gh pr list --search \"path alias\\|oldText alias\\|parameter\" --json number,title,state 2>/dev/null | head -20","timeout":15}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":86553,"output":459,"cacheRead":21632,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":108644,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774868861346,"responseId":"06198c7dbaedd6b69e792e84f3b88565"}}
{"type":"message","id":"fadd1df1","parentId":"718ca74b","timestamp":"2026-03-30T11:07:54.989Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_function_nuvmvpt1jbxe_1","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[{\"number\":48670,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"edit/write/read: add tool-specific hints to parameter validation errors\"},{\"number\":57554,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"fix: tool parameter validation diagnostics and read path alias coverage\"},{\"number\":37677,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"fix(tools): replace original param name with alias in required array for Claude compat patch\"},{\"number\":50232,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"fix: accept camelCase edit tool aliases\"},{\"number\":42580,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"feat(tools): add old_text/new_text parameter aliases for edit tool\"},{\"number\":27477,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"fix: normalize edit aliases and stabilize mutation fingerprint\"},{\"number\":48792,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"fix(tools): strip arg_value XML suffix from exec/read args\"}]"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":533,"aggregated":"[{\"number\":48670,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"edit/write/read: add tool-specific hints to parameter validation errors\"},{\"number\":57554,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"fix: tool parameter validation diagnostics and read path alias coverage\"},{\"number\":37677,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"fix(tools): replace original param name with alias in required array for Claude compat patch\"},{\"number\":50232,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"fix: accept camelCase edit tool aliases\"},{\"number\":42580,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"feat(tools): add old_text/new_text parameter aliases for edit tool\"},{\"number\":27477,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"fix: normalize edit aliases and stabilize mutation fingerprint\"},{\"number\":48792,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"fix(tools): strip arg_value XML suffix from exec/read args\"}]","cwd":"/root/.openclaw/workspace"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1774868874964}}
{"type":"message","id":"8c057b97","parentId":"fadd1df1","timestamp":"2026-03-30T11:08:03.405Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"PR #57554 seems very relevant - \"fix: tool parameter validation diagnostics and read path alias coverage\". Let me check if this is the fix for the edit tool parameter issue. Also, #50232 \"fix: accept camelCase edit tool aliases\" seems relevant.\n\nActually, looking at the error message more carefully: \"Missing required parameters: path alias, oldText alias, newText alias\" - this looks like the error is showing parameter names with \"alias\" appended. This might mean:\n1. The tool schema has `path` as a parameter name, but there's also an alias\n2. The error message is showing both the parameter name and the alias\n3. When the tool call is made, it's using the alias names but the server is expecting the parameter names\n\n\n4. The fix would need to align which form is being sent with what the server actually expects\n\nLet me look into PR #57554 to see if it's already addressing this specific issue.","thinkingSignature":"5d2f1b2fa988afa5c8852d9a194d1d2061db8728ff246e0f078f1c12f46c0729"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_22rdtlcdskvl_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/.openclaw/workspace/openclaw && gh pr view 57554 --json body,title,files,state 2>&1 | head -60","timeout":15}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":87197,"output":264,"cacheRead":21632,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":109093,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774868874991,"responseId":"06198c8b3d2b20b54ac8a03218e4ab67"}}
{"type":"message","id":"61e56636","parentId":"8c057b97","timestamp":"2026-03-30T11:08:03.968Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_function_22rdtlcdskvl_1","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"body\":\"## Summary\\n\\n- `assertRequiredParams` now logs the actual keys and value types received when validation fails, making it possible to diagnose model tool-call schema mismatches from the error message alone\\n- The read tool \\\"called without path\\\" warning now checks all 4 path aliases (`path`, `file_path`, `filePath`, `file`) instead of just 2, eliminating false-alarm warnings when the model uses `filePath` or `file`\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nRunning Claude Opus 4.6 via the embedded agent, the model intermittently sends edit tool calls with parameter keys that don't match any recognized alias. The current error message (`Missing required parameters: oldText alias, newText alias`) doesn't include what was actually received, making it impossible to diagnose without adding temporary debug logging. This PR makes the error self-documenting.\\n\\nThe read path warning was checking only `path` and `file_path` but missing `filePath` and `file`, which are valid aliases accepted by `normalizeToolParams` and `CLAUDE_PARAM_GROUPS.read`. This caused spurious warnings in the gateway log even though the read tool itself would succeed.\\n\\n## Test plan\\n\\n- [ ] Trigger an edit tool call with invalid parameter names and verify the error message now includes `Received keys: [key:type, ...]`\\n- [ ] Trigger a read tool call using `filePath` as the key and verify no false warning is logged\\n- [ ] Existing tests pass (`pnpm test`)\\n\\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\",\"files\":[{\"path\":\"src/agents/pi-embedded-subscribe.handlers.tools.ts\",\"additions\":5,\"deletions\":1,\"changeType\":\"MODIFIED\"},{\"path\":\"src/agents/pi-tools.params.ts\",\"additions\":5,\"deletions\":1,\"changeType\":\"MODIFIED\"}],\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"fix: tool parameter validation diagnostics and read path alias coverage\"}"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":513,"aggregated":"{\"body\":\"## Summary\\n\\n- `assertRequiredParams` now logs the actual keys and value types received when validation fails, making it possible to diagnose model tool-call schema mismatches from the error message alone\\n- The read tool \\\"called without path\\\" warning now checks all 4 path aliases (`path`, `file_path`, `filePath`, `file`) instead of just 2, eliminating false-alarm warnings when the model uses `filePath` or `file`\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nRunning Claude Opus 4.6 via the embedded agent, the model intermittently sends edit tool calls with parameter keys that don't match any recognized alias. The current error message (`Missing required parameters: oldText alias, newText alias`) doesn't include what was actually received, making it impossible to diagnose without adding temporary debug logging. This PR makes the error self-documenting.\\n\\nThe read path warning was checking only `path` and `file_path` but missing `filePath` and `file`, which are valid aliases accepted by `normalizeToolParams` and `CLAUDE_PARAM_GROUPS.read`. This caused spurious warnings in the gateway log even though the read tool itself would succeed.\\n\\n## Test plan\\n\\n- [ ] Trigger an edit tool call with invalid parameter names and verify the error message now includes `Received keys: [key:type, ...]`\\n- [ ] Trigger a read tool call using `filePath` as the key and verify no false warning is logged\\n- [ ] Existing tests pass (`pnpm test`)\\n\\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\",\"files\":[{\"path\":\"src/agents/pi-embedded-subscribe.handlers.tools.ts\",\"additions\":5,\"deletions\":1,\"changeType\":\"MODIFIED\"},{\"path\":\"src/agents/pi-tools.params.ts\",\"additions\":5,\"deletions\":1,\"changeType\":\"MODIFIED\"}],\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"fix: tool parameter validation diagnostics and read path alias coverage\"}","cwd":"/root/.openclaw/workspace"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1774868883940}}
{"type":"message","id":"77239ab9","parentId":"61e56636","timestamp":"2026-03-30T11:08:15.478Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"PR #57554 is a DIAGNOSTICS improvement (better error messages) - it doesn't fix the root cause of the infinite retry. The issue is that the edit tool is being called with incorrect parameter names, and the system retries with the same incorrect parameters infinitely.\n\nThe root cause is likely that the model is sending parameter names that don't match the tool schema, or the parameters are being mangled somewhere in the pipeline. This looks like the same issue as #57334.\n\nLet me think about this differently:\n1. The model sends an edit tool call with some parameters\n2. The parameters get corrupted/mangled somewhere\n3. The server receives wrong parameter names (like \"path alias\" instead of \"path\")\n4. Validation fails with \"Missing required parameters\"\n5. The system retries with the same corrupted parameters → infinite loop\n\nThe fix should be either:\n1. Make the validation more lenient (accept \"path alias\" as equivalent to \"path\") - but this is wrong\n2. Fix the parameter serialization/deserialization to not corrupt the parameter names\n3. Add a check: if a tool call fails validation, don't retry with the same parameters - this is the right approach\n\n\n4. The retry logic is blindly re-attempting with parameters that failed validation the first time, which guarantees the same failure will happen again\n\nSo the real fix needs to prevent retrying failed tool calls - if validation fails, the system shouldn't just blindly retry with the identical corrupted parameters. But this is a major architectural change. I should look at the existing PRs and issues to see if someone's already addressed this, particularly #57334 which seemed similar.","thinkingSignature":"ee04146986882aadb19243473f5a00cfa4263674a8bdee672a914b10ef1ec9ba"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_gl3dox5fxd39_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/.openclaw/workspace/openclaw && gh issue view 57334 --json body,title 2>&1 | head -40","timeout":15}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":87894,"output":397,"cacheRead":21632,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":109923,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774868883970,"responseId":"06198c94462dddf18d2915fc2667c1b6"}}
{"type":"message","id":"8954d4bf","parentId":"77239ab9","timestamp":"2026-03-30T11:08:16.058Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_function_gl3dox5fxd39_1","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"body\":\"### Bug type\\n\\nRegression (worked before, now fails)\\n\\n### Beta release blocker\\n\\nNo\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\nThe `listSessionFilesForAgent` function in `query-expansion-CeNhqo71.js` filters session files with `.filter((name) => name.endsWith(\\\".jsonl\\\"))`, which excludes `.jsonl.reset.*` and `.jsonl.deleted.*` files. After the daily 4 AM session reset, main session transcripts are renamed from `<id>.jsonl` to `<id>.jsonl.reset.<timestamp>` — making them invisible to the session indexer. Only small cron/subagent sessions (which aren't reset) and the current live session remain as `.jsonl`. Result: 211 `.jsonl` files indexed out of 485 total, with all large main conversation sessions excluded.\\n\\n### Steps to reproduce\\n\\n1. Configure an agent with `sources: [\\\"memory\\\", \\\"sessions\\\"]` and `experimental.sessionMemory: true`.\\n2. Use the agent actively for several days with the daily 4 AM session reset enabled.\\n3. Run `ls ~/.openclaw/agents/main/sessions/ | grep -c '\\\\.jsonl$'` (active files) vs `ls | wc -l` (total files).\\n4. Run `openclaw memory index --force --verbose` and note the session chunk count.\\n5. Run `sqlite3 ~/.openclaw/memory/main.sqlite \\\"SELECT path, COUNT(*) FROM chunks WHERE source='sessions' GROUP BY path ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC LIMIT 10\\\"` — only small cron sessions appear.\\n\\n**Source code reference:** `query-expansion-CeNhqo71.js`, line ~413:\\n```js\\n.filter((name) => name.endsWith(\\\".jsonl\\\"))\\n```\\n\\n### Expected behavior\\n\\nAll session transcript files — including `.jsonl.reset.*` and `.jsonl.deleted.*` — should be included in session indexing. These are completed session transcripts that represent the bulk of conversation history. The file filter should match `.jsonl` anywhere in the filename, not just at the end. e.g. `.filter((name) => name.includes(\\\".jsonl\\\"))`\\n\\n### Actual behavior\\n\\nOnly 211 out of 485 session files are indexed. The 274 excluded files include all main conversation sessions (the largest and most valuable transcripts). A 13.7MB main session with 416 user/assistant messages (~214K chars of conversation text, estimated ~133 chunks) produces 0 indexed chunks because it was renamed to `.jsonl.reset.*` after the 4 AM session reset.\\n\\nThe indexed sessions are almost entirely small cron/subagent sessions (1-10 chunks each), giving the false impression that the chunker is \\\"sparse\\\" when in reality the large sessions simply aren't being read.\\n\\n```\\n# Our deployment:\\nTotal files in sessions dir: 485\\nFiles ending in .jsonl: 211 (small cron/subagent sessions)\\nFiles ending in .jsonl.reset.*: 57 (main conversation sessions — EXCLUDED)\\nFiles ending in .jsonl.deleted.*: 217 (archived sessions — EXCLUDED)\\n\\n# Database shows:\\nCurrent live session: 173 chunks (correctly indexed)\\nSecond largest: 10 chunks (small cron session)\\nAll main past sessions: 0 chunks (invisible)\\n```\\n\\n### OpenClaw version\\n\\n2026.3.24 (cff6dc9) — also confirmed in 2026.3.28 source\\n\\n### Operating system\\n\\nmacOS 25.3.0 (Mac mini M4, arm64)\\n\\n### Install method\\n\\nnpm global\\n\\n### Model\\n\\nN/A — indexing bug, not model-specific\\n\\n### Provider / routing chain\\n\\nN/A — indexing bug\\n\\n### Additional provider/model setup details\\n\\n_No response_\\n\\n### Logs, screenshots, and evidence\\n\\n```shell\\n\\n```\\n\\n### Impact and severity\\n\\n**Affected:** Any user with daily session resets enabled (the default behavior). All main conversation sessions become invisible to memory-core search after the reset renames them.\\n**Severity:** High — effectively disables cross-session memory recall via the native session indexer. Users believe session indexing is working (status shows `sources: [\\\"memory\\\", \\\"sessions\\\"]`) but past conversations are silently excluded.\\n**Frequency:** 100% — every session reset renames the file, every renamed file is excluded.\\n**Consequence:** `memory_search` cannot find content from any past main session. Only cron/subagent sessions and the current live session are searchable. This defeats the purpose of `sources: [\\\"sessions\\\"]`.\\n\\n### Additional information\\n\\nRelated to #44028 (shouldSyncSessions bug) — we initially attributed the sparse session chunk count (380 chunks from 204 files) to aggressive chunking. The real cause is that the 204 `.jsonl` files are mostly tiny cron sessions. The large main sessions were never in the candidate pool because they'd been renamed to `.reset` or `.deleted`.\\n\\nOur workaround: a custom `transcript-to-md.py` script that converts ALL session files (including `.reset` and `.deleted`) to markdown, which memory-core then indexes as `source=\\\"memory\\\"` files with proper chunking (~24 chunks per file). This works but should not be necessary — the native session indexer should handle renamed files.\\n\\n**Suggested fix:** Change the file filter from `name.endsWith(\\\".jsonl\\\")` to `name.includes(\\\".jsonl\\\")` (or a regex like `/\\\\.jsonl(\\\\.|$)/`), so `.jsonl.reset.*` and `.jsonl.deleted.*` files are included in session indexing.\",\"title\":\"[Bug]: Memory-core session indexer skips .jsonl.reset and .jsonl.deleted files — most past sessions invisible to search\"}"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":479,"aggregated":"{\"body\":\"### Bug type\\n\\nRegression (worked before, now fails)\\n\\n### Beta release blocker\\n\\nNo\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\nThe `listSessionFilesForAgent` function in `query-expansion-CeNhqo71.js` filters session files with `.filter((name) => name.endsWith(\\\".jsonl\\\"))`, which excludes `.jsonl.reset.*` and `.jsonl.deleted.*` files. After the daily 4 AM session reset, main session transcripts are renamed from `<id>.jsonl` to `<id>.jsonl.reset.<timestamp>` — making them invisible to the session indexer. Only small cron/subagent sessions (which aren't reset) and the current live session remain as `.jsonl`. Result: 211 `.jsonl` files indexed out of 485 total, with all large main conversation sessions excluded.\\n\\n### Steps to reproduce\\n\\n1. Configure an agent with `sources: [\\\"memory\\\", \\\"sessions\\\"]` and `experimental.sessionMemory: true`.\\n2. Use the agent actively for several days with the daily 4 AM session reset enabled.\\n3. Run `ls ~/.openclaw/agents/main/sessions/ | grep -c '\\\\.jsonl$'` (active files) vs `ls | wc -l` (total files).\\n4. Run `openclaw memory index --force --verbose` and note the session chunk count.\\n5. Run `sqlite3 ~/.openclaw/memory/main.sqlite \\\"SELECT path, COUNT(*) FROM chunks WHERE source='sessions' GROUP BY path ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC LIMIT 10\\\"` — only small cron sessions appear.\\n\\n**Source code reference:** `query-expansion-CeNhqo71.js`, line ~413:\\n```js\\n.filter((name) => name.endsWith(\\\".jsonl\\\"))\\n```\\n\\n### Expected behavior\\n\\nAll session transcript files — including `.jsonl.reset.*` and `.jsonl.deleted.*` — should be included in session indexing. These are completed session transcripts that represent the bulk of conversation history. The file filter should match `.jsonl` anywhere in the filename, not just at the end. e.g. `.filter((name) => name.includes(\\\".jsonl\\\"))`\\n\\n### Actual behavior\\n\\nOnly 211 out of 485 session files are indexed. The 274 excluded files include all main conversation sessions (the largest and most valuable transcripts). A 13.7MB main session with 416 user/assistant messages (~214K chars of conversation text, estimated ~133 chunks) produces 0 indexed chunks because it was renamed to `.jsonl.reset.*` after the 4 AM session reset.\\n\\nThe indexed sessions are almost entirely small cron/subagent sessions (1-10 chunks each), giving the false impression that the chunker is \\\"sparse\\\" when in reality the large sessions simply aren't being read.\\n\\n```\\n# Our deployment:\\nTotal files in sessions dir: 485\\nFiles ending in .jsonl: 211 (small cron/subagent sessions)\\nFiles ending in .jsonl.reset.*: 57 (main conversation sessions — EXCLUDED)\\nFiles ending in .jsonl.deleted.*: 217 (archived sessions — EXCLUDED)\\n\\n# Database shows:\\nCurrent live session: 173 chunks (correctly indexed)\\nSecond largest: 10 chunks (small cron session)\\nAll main past sessions: 0 chunks (invisible)\\n```\\n\\n### OpenClaw version\\n\\n2026.3.24 (cff6dc9) — also confirmed in 2026.3.28 source\\n\\n### Operating system\\n\\nmacOS 25.3.0 (Mac mini M4, arm64)\\n\\n### Install method\\n\\nnpm global\\n\\n### Model\\n\\nN/A — indexing bug, not model-specific\\n\\n### Provider / routing chain\\n\\nN/A — indexing bug\\n\\n### Additional provider/model setup details\\n\\n_No response_\\n\\n### Logs, screenshots, and evidence\\n\\n```shell\\n\\n```\\n\\n### Impact and severity\\n\\n**Affected:** Any user with daily session resets enabled (the default behavior). All main conversation sessions become invisible to memory-core search after the reset renames them.\\n**Severity:** High — effectively disables cross-session memory recall via the native session indexer. Users believe session indexing is working (status shows `sources: [\\\"memory\\\", \\\"sessions\\\"]`) but past conversations are silently excluded.\\n**Frequency:** 100% — every session reset renames the file, every renamed file is excluded.\\n**Consequence:** `memory_search` cannot find content from any past main session. Only cron/subagent sessions and the current live session are searchable. This defeats the purpose of `sources: [\\\"sessions\\\"]`.\\n\\n### Additional information\\n\\nRelated to #44028 (shouldSyncSessions bug) — we initially attributed the sparse session chunk count (380 chunks from 204 files) to aggressive chunking. The real cause is that the 204 `.jsonl` files are mostly tiny cron sessions. The large main sessions were never in the candidate pool because they'd been renamed to `.reset` or `.deleted`.\\n\\nOur workaround: a custom `transcript-to-md.py` script that converts ALL session files (including `.reset` and `.deleted`) to markdown, which memory-core then indexes as `source=\\\"memory\\\"` files with proper chunking (~24 chunks per file). This works but should not be necessary — the native session indexer should handle renamed files.\\n\\n**Suggested fix:** Change the file filter from `name.endsWith(\\\".jsonl\\\")` to `name.includes(\\\".jsonl\\\")` (or a regex like `/\\\\.jsonl(\\\\.|$)/`), so `.jsonl.reset.*` and `.jsonl.deleted.*` files are included in session indexing.\",\"title\":\"[Bug]: Memory-core session indexer skips .jsonl.reset and .jsonl.deleted files — most past sessions invisible to search\"}","cwd":"/root/.openclaw/workspace"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1774868896035}}
{"type":"message","id":"503f3fc6","parentId":"8954d4bf","timestamp":"2026-03-30T11:08:26.549Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"#57334 is about memory-core session indexer, not the edit tool parameter issue. It's different from #57551.\n\nNow let me think about what the actual #57551 fix should be. The problem is:\n1. User calls edit tool with parameters\n2. The parameters fail validation: \"Missing required parameters: path alias, oldText alias, newText alias\"\n3. The system retries the same tool call → same error → infinite loop\n\nThe error message shows parameter NAMES like \"path alias\" instead of actual values. This is very weird. It looks like the parameters are being serialized incorrectly.\n\nBut regardless of the root cause of the parameter corruption, the immediate fix should be: **don't retry a tool call that failed due to parameter validation errors**. If the parameters are wrong, retrying with the same parameters won't help.\n\n\nThe real issue is in the retry logic—it keeps attempting the same failed parameters instead of distinguishing between transient errors (network timeout) and permanent ones (bad parameters). I should create a minimal reproduction case to trace where parameters get corrupted, but since aoao keeps timing out, I'll focus on identifying and applying the right fix first. Looking at the existing PRs like #57554, the validation error improvements won't solve the infinite retry problem, so I need to examine the actual error handling code to understand where the retry loop is triggered.","thinkingSignature":"0a218fedc9095ba946bd1866b2138881a658e8b84bc2b1ae3e479deb4f9a419c"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_54fhcx6ps2a7_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/.openclaw/workspace/openclaw && grep -rn \"Missing required parameters\" src/ 2>/dev/null | head -5","timeout":10}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":89575,"output":341,"cacheRead":21632,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":111548,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774868896064,"responseId":"06198ca0eab87da0f84b9f5cbd2535e4"}}
